From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:02:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 134/135] kk: cmd_retire destroys idle sealed allocators, caps
 a pool at eight and begins Metal at the first encoder

With MESA_KK_EXPERIMENTAL=cmd_retire (0132) RDR2's command allocators
went from 74 to 190-252 and 1.2-1.9 GB in its cabin's load step, and
the process was ended for memory. Hollow Knight destroyed and made one
allocator a frame. The causes, in the code:

- A Metal reset keeps an allocator's heaps (MTL4CommandAllocator.h:
  reset lets new command buffers "reuse its existing internal memory
  heaps"; allocatedSize is the heaps' size). An allocator sealed past
  8 MiB was reset at a Begin and sealed again at its next End, so it
  took one recording at a time, never counted among the pool's four
  unsealed allocators and was never destroyed: every allocator that
  once passed 8 MiB let the pool make another, and none went away.
- A ONE_TIME recording begun and ended but never submitted tainted its
  allocator although it encoded nothing: DXVK begins the next command
  list's spare command buffer after each submission and submits it
  only if it was used (dxvk_cmdlist.cpp next(), submit()), once a frame.

With cmd_retire now:

- A recording begins its Metal command buffer at its first encoder
  (kk_cmd_begin_metal), not at vkBeginCommandBuffer. One that encodes
  nothing (DXVK's spare buffer, a secondary, an empty re-record) never
  begins one: it is not committed and leaves its allocator clean
  (unbegun=). One that began and is released uncommitted still taints
  its allocator, which is destroyed, never reset.
- An idle (no users) sealed allocator is destroyed, not reset: its
  memory goes back. Of the idle clean ones the first is reset and taken
  and up to three more are kept; the rest are destroyed (trimmed=).
- A pool makes at most KK_CMD_POOL_ALLOCATOR_CAP (8) allocators. At the
  cap a recording shares the least-filled unsealed ended allocator, or
  if there is none the least-filled sealed one (capped=), which is
  destroyed once idle. Only when every allocator records now or is
  tainted is a new one made past the cap (over-cap=): a Metal
  allocator serves one command buffer at a time. Nothing waits for the
  GPU.
- The idle scan reads no allocatedSize; only a Begin that finds none
  idle compares sizes, to share.
- [kk-cmd-retire] adds released= (sealed ones destroyed), trimmed=,
  capped=, over-cap=, pool-max= (the most allocators one pool had) and
  unbegun=.

Without the switch Begin begins the command buffer and nothing else
changes. kk-cmd-retire-test.c checks it on the mock, whose reset now
keeps the size and which fails a reset of an allocator a command
buffer was begun on and not committed.

Linear: PLA-364
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-30-rdr2-kk-audio-diagnostics.md
Offered-upstream: no
